          <p>
            <strong>Z1.</strong> The same idea, newer and $200 cheaper, with a slightly higher top speed and
            a lighter frame. It gives up width and weight rating. For a walker under about 240 lb it is the
            better value; our <a href="/walkingpad-z1-review/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">WalkingPad Z1 review</a>
            sets out its figures and the motor and noise claims that do not hold up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>C2.</strong> Close to the Z1 on figures, $100 more, with colour choices and desk
            bundles. Buy it for those reasons rather than for the hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>R3.</strong> Amazon describes the R3 family as a newer model than the A1 Pro, but it is a
            different kind of machine: a wider, faster pad that does not fold in half. WalkingPad listed the
            R3 at $649 for a 265 lb version and $699 for a 330 lb version on 5 November. If you want a little
            jogging and have room to store a full-length deck, it is worth a look; if folding is the point,
            it is not a replacement.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pads from other brands, including incline models and ones with a handrail, are ranked in our
