        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Buying a WalkingPad A1 Pro Secondhand</h2>
<p>
            Because the A1 Pro has been on sale for six years, a lot of them are on resale sites. A used
            one can be good value, but a walking pad’s warranty rarely transfers, and the parts that wear
            are the belt, the deck and the motor. Check these before you hand over money:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>The weight rating on the label.</strong> The label under or behind the deck should say whether it is the 230 lb or the 300 lb version. Sellers often do not know.</li>
            <li><strong>Run it at full speed for several minutes.</strong> Listen for grinding or a rising whine, and watch for the belt hesitating when you step on. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt that slows when you step on it</a> explains what that means.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the belt for fraying and drift.</strong> A belt that wanders to one side needs adjusting and may have been neglected.</li>
            <li><strong>Fold and unfold it.</strong> The hinge should lock firmly with no play.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask for the remote and the safety items.</strong> A missing remote means relying on the app.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask about lubrication.</strong> Walking pads need occasional silicone under the belt; our <a href="/how-to-lubricate-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad lubrication guide</a> covers how and how often.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            On price, a used A1 Pro only makes sense well below WalkingPad’s $499 new price, because a new
            Z1 is $299 with a warranty. Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            covers the general checks, and <a href="/how-long-do-walking-pads-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long walking pads last</a>
            helps you judge how much life an older one has left.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy the WalkingPad A1 Pro</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if folding storage is essential and you weigh more than the Z1 allows.</strong>
            The 300 lb version’s rating and the 16.5-inch belt are the real reasons to pay $200 more than
            the Z1.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the Z1 instead</strong> if you are under about 240 lb and want the same fold for
            less, or if you will move the pad every day and want it lighter.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look beyond WalkingPad</strong> if you want incline, a handrail, a top speed above
            4 mph or a weight rating above 300 lb; our roundups of
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with incline</a>
            and <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with a handrail</a>
            cover those.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it at $499 or less.</strong> WalkingPad’s $699 reference price is not what it sells
            for, and our <a href="/black-friday-walking-pad-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad Black Friday guide</a>
            explains how to judge a November discount.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the WalkingPad A1 Pro discontinued?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not by WalkingPad. On 5 November 2026 WalkingPad’s US store listed the A1 Pro as in stock at $499 against a $699 reference price. Best Buy no longer sells it new and some Amazon listings have gone, so availability elsewhere is patchy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit of the WalkingPad A1 Pro?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the version. The A1 Pro is sold in a 230 lb and a 300 lb version, and on 5 November 2026 WalkingPad’s main listing offered only the 300 lb one. Check the listing and the label on the machine. One long-term review suggests the lower figure is the more realistic one for durability.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How fast does the WalkingPad A1 Pro go?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                WalkingPad lists 0.5 to 3.75 mph (6 km/h); some listings round it to 3.7 mph. It is a walking machine with no incline. One review reports that the top speed is only available after pairing the pad with the KS Fit app.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What motor does the WalkingPad A1 Pro have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sources disagree. WalkingPad’s current page calls it a 3.75 HP brushless motor, Best Buy lists 1.25 HP, and an independent tracker reports 2 HP on Amazon. Because the figures conflict we do not quote one, and at walking speeds the rating matters less than the weight limit and warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">WalkingPad A1 Pro vs Z1: which should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both fold in half. The A1 Pro has a slightly wider 16.5-inch walking area and a 300 lb version; the Z1 is about 12 lb lighter, reaches 4 mph and is rated at 242 lb. On 5 November 2026 WalkingPad sold the A1 Pro at $499 and the Z1 at $299, so the Z1 is the better value for walkers under about 240 lb.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the WalkingPad A1 Pro need assembly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. WalkingPad lists it as fully assembled. Unfold it, plug it into a 110 V outlet, and pair the remote or the KS Fit app.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a WalkingPad A1 Pro</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The A1 Pro is simple to buy, but these errors catch people out because of its age and its two versions.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking which weight version you are buying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The 230 lb and 300 lb versions share a name. If you are over about 220 lb, confirm 300 lb on the listing, the box and the machine label before paying.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a pooled star rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Amazon pools reviews for several WalkingPad models on one listing, so a rating shown next to the A1 Pro may be about the Z1 or P1. Read the reviews themselves, or an independent review.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying close to the reference price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">WalkingPad shows $699 as the reference and sold it at $499 on 5 November 2026. A third-party listing near $699 is not a bargain, and a used one should cost well under $499.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a fold-in-half pad when you do not need the fold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The hinge is the reason to buy an A1 Pro and the part you feel underfoot. If you have room to store a flat pad, a single-piece deck may be cheaper and feel more solid.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

